Why does regularization help with overfitting? Why does it help with reducing variance problems?

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.420
Let's go through a couple examples to gain some intuition about how it works.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:15.940
So recall that our high bias, high variance, and

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.940
I just write

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.420
pictures from earlier video had looked something like this.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.300
Now let's see if fitting a large and deep neural network.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:34.300
I know I haven't drawn this one too large or too deep, but let's see if things on your network and it's currently overfitting.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.620
So you have some cost function, right? J of

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:43.940
WB equals sum of the losses

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.420
like so.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:52.900
Right? And so what we did for regularization was add this extra term

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:57.050
that

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:58.330
penalizes

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:01.690
the weight matrices from being too large.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:04.250
And we said that was a Frobenius norm.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.250
So why is it that

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:12.370
shrinking the L2 norm or the Frobenius norm with the parameters might cause less overfitting?

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:17.410
One piece of intuition is that if you, you know, crank the regularization lambda to be really really big,

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:23.570
that'll be really incentivized to set the weight matrices W to be reasonably close to zero.

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.450
So one piece of intuition is

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:32.210
maybe it'll set the weights to be so close to zero for a lot of hidden units. There's basically

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:39.330
zeroing out a lot of the impact of these hidden units. And if that's the case, then you know this

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:44.730
much simplified neural network becomes a much smaller neural network.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:49.530
In fact, it's almost like a logistic regression unit, you know, a bit stacked multiple layers deep.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:53.010
And so that will take you from this overfitting case

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:57.050
much closer to the left to the high bias case.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:03.890
But hopefully there'll be an intermediate value of lambda that results in the result closer to this just right case in the middle.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:09.930
But the intuition is that by cranking up lambda to be really big, it'll set W close to zero,

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:13.970
which in practice, this isn't actually what happens. We can think of it as

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:18.690
zeroing out or at least reducing the impact of a lot of hidden units.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:23.450
So you end up with what might feel like a simpler network that gets closer and closer.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.010
So as if you were just using which is super regression.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:31.210
The intuition of completely zeroing out a bunch of hidden units isn't quite right.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:35.210
It turns out that what actually happens is it'll still use all the hidden units,

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:37.290
but each of them will just have a much smaller effect.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:43.490
But you do end up with a simpler network and as if you have a smaller network that is therefore

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.010
less prone to overfitting.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:50.850
So I'm not sure if this intuition helps, but when you implement regularization in the programming exercise,

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:55.010
you actually see some of these variance reduction results yourself.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:03:01.570
Here's another attempt at additional intuition for why regularization helps prevent overfitting.

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:08.490
And for this, I'm going to assume that we're using the tanh activation function, which looks like this.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:11.770
Right. So this is a G of Z equals tanh of Z.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:18.410
So if that's the case, notice that so long as Z is quite small.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:24.410
So if Z takes on only a smallest range of parameters, maybe around here,

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:28.050
then you're just using the linear regime of the tanh function.

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:33.890
It's only if Z is allowed to wander out to larger values or smaller values like so,

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:37.450
that the activation function starts to become less linear.

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:40.530
So the intuition you might take away from this is that if lambda,

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:46.610
the regularization parameters launch, then you have that your parameters will be relatively small

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:51.090
because they are penalized to be large in the cost function.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:04:00.620
And so if the weights W are small, then because Z is equal to W, right,

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:06.820
and then technically is buzz B, but if W tends to be very small, then Z will also be relatively small.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:12.460
And in particular, if Z ends up taking relatively small values just in this little range,

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.940
then G of Z will be roughly linear.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:24.610
So it's as if every layer will be roughly linear, as if it's just linear regression.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.610
And we saw in course one that if every layer is linear,

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:31.210
then your whole network is just a linear network.

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:36.290
And so even a very deep network with a deep network with a linear activation function is,

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:38.930
at the end of the day, only able to compute a linear function.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:43.410
So it's not able to fit those very, very complicated decision,

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:51.570
very non-linear decision boundaries that allow it to really overfit the data sets,

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:57.010
like we saw on the overfitting high variance case on the previous slide.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:01.490
So just to summarize, if the regularization parameters are very large,

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:06.370
the parameters W very small, so Z will be relatively small,

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:08.250
kind of ignoring the effects of B for now.

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:13.570
But so Z is relatively, so Z would be relatively small, or really,

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:15.930
you should say it takes on a small range of values.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:21.530
And so the activation function, if it's 10HZ, will be relatively linear.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:27.410
And so your whole neural network will be computing something not too far from a big linear function,

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:30.730
which is therefore a pretty simple function rather than a very complex,

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:32.010
highly non-linear function.

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.330
And so it's also much less able to overfit.

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:38.690
And again, when you implement regularization for yourself in the current exercise,

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:41.410
you'll be able to see some of these effects yourself.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:45.770
Before wrapping up our discussion on regularization,

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:49.370
I just want to give you one implementation tip, which is that

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:54.650
when implementing regularization, we took our definition of the cost function,

00:05:55.530 --> 00:06:04.250
J, and we actually modified it by adding this extra term that penalizes the

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:05.770
weights being too large.

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:12.530
And so if you implement gradient descent, one of the steps to debug gradient

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:19.490
descent is to plot the cost function J as a function of the number of

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:21.090
iterations of gradient descent.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:26.250
And you want to see that the cost function J decreases monotonically after every

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:27.530
iteration of gradient descent.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:33.770
And if you're implementing regularization, then please remember that J now has this

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:34.770
new definition.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:40.610
If you plot the old definition of J, just this first term, then you might not see a

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:42.010
decreased monotonically.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:46.730
So to debug gradient descent, make sure that you're plotting this new definition of J

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:49.250
that includes the second term as well.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:53.890
Otherwise, you might not see J decrease monotonically on every single iteration.

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:58.570
So that's it for L2 regularization, which is actually a regularization technique that

